Personally, I prefer the opposite approach,
descending at lowest possible XL to minimize monster
difficulty until I've paid for protection and
cleaned out the Sokoban top level (which is usually
a piece of cake if you arrive there with two buff
pets and your PC at XL 1). Going down with minimal
XL, you won't see team ant or mumaks in Mines Town,
though what is there takes lots of pet management
skills and running the opposite direction from
danger skills, indeed. Mines Town caters for that
approach, since there are several chunks you can run
around in circles, and the monster move algorithm is
so incredibly brain dead that they just keep
following you while your pet snipes them off one by
one from behind.

[DevTeam: you could make NetHack tenfold better a
game just by smartening up the monsters enough that
groups of monsters move more cleverly: attack in a
row abreast instead of in single column formation,
send scouts around to cut off PC retreat or just
attack from other directions, melee in coordination
to split damage received while combining damage
dealt, do a little dancing out of danger themselves,
around trees and boulders, and so on.]

The point Damerell misses is that the descent
strategy is a _personal choice_. Anyone who thinks
there is one best way to play a game as complex as
NetHack and by players with a widespread kit of game
play preferences is delusional.

I don't kill peacefuls, don't do alchemy, rarely
wish for artifacts, rarely if ever write scrolls or
spell-books, can't remember the last time I genocided
anything. None of these are what NetHack _is_, to
me.

I dilute almost all potions for holy water.

I also assiduously try on armor to _find_ good stuff
rather than needing to enchant it, and I have a long
list of other game habits. I may stop and dig out
whole levels just for fun, if my food supply is
high. I may altar camp once in 20 games, pudding
farm once in a hundred games (I used to do it in
every game where it was possible; moods change), but
I have to be in a really placid mood to do either.

You play NetHack the way that matches your skills
and habits as a player, for best results.

None of my habits have ever let me ascend (ascension
looks way too boring), but all have kept the game
fresh, fun and challenging _for me_ for lo these two
decades now.

So, my strategy for how to manage pets and XL while
descending has to be chosen to match the rest of how
I play the game.  Playing at weak XL also engenders
a sense of caution, which helps a lot in making me a
better NetHack player, since impatient play by me,
rather than any particular monster or other
dangerous game feature,  is far and away the single
biggest cause of death to my PCs.




